{"tb-3B04-1":{"number":"1","question":"<p><i><b>Which of the following descriptions about the </b></i><i><b>period of a transverse wave is </b></i><i><b>incorrect</b></i><i><b>?</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>A The time taken to produce one complete </b></i><i><b>cycle of wave</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>B The time taken for a trough to travel one </b></i><i><b>wavelength</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>C The time taken for a particle to travel four </b></i><i><b>amplitudes</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>D The time taken for a particle to return to its </b></i><i><b>equilibrium position</b></i></p>","answer":"<p>D</p>","reasoning":"<p>The period T is the time for one complete oscillation, also the time for a wave feature to travel one wavelength. A particle travels a total distance 4A in one complete oscillation.</p><p>A particle can return to equilibrium after half a period, moving in the opposite direction. Returning to equilibrium alone does not define a complete cycle. D is incorrect.</p>","kind":"multiple-choice","source":{"name":"3B04 Revision 4 Nature of Waves.docx","sha256":"ce4955eaa02f2dfff1d869d1830f48153016d479791ee42b406abae452601c85","block":3},"id":"tb-3B04-1","chapter":"3B04"},"tb-3B04-2":{"number":"2","question":"<p><i><b>The </b></i><i><b>s</b></i><i><b>–</b></i><i><b>d </b></i><i><b>graph of a wave at a certain instant is </b></i><i><b>shown in Figure a.</b></i></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-exercises/media/0f4efc4af39af3320b6635ba.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:312.4px\"/></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>If the wave travels at 20 m s</b></i><i><b><sup>–1</sup></b></i><i><b>, what is its period?</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>A 0.0125 s </b></i></p><p><i><b>B 0.015 s </b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>C 0.025 s </b></i></p><p><i><b>D 0.0275 s</b></i></p>","answer":"<p>C</p>","reasoning":"<p>  <i>λ</i> = 0.55 – 0.05 = 0.5 m</p><p>  By <i>v</i> =<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>λ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></math>, </p><p>    20  =<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0.5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></math></p><p>    <i>T</i>  = 0.025 s</p>","kind":"multiple-choice","source":{"name":"3B04 Revision 4 Nature of Waves.docx","sha256":"ce4955eaa02f2dfff1d869d1830f48153016d479791ee42b406abae452601c85","block":10},"id":"tb-3B04-2","chapter":"3B04"},"tb-3B04-3":{"number":"3","question":"<p><i><b>Which of the following statements about a travelling </b></i><i><b>wave </b></i><i><b>must be</b></i><i><b> correct?</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(1) The particles vibrate in the direction </b></i><i><b>perpendicular to the direction of travel of the </b></i><i><b>wave.</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(2) It can be described as vibrations.</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(3) No matter is transferred by the wave.</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>A (1) only </b></i></p><p><i><b>B (3) only</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>C (1) and (3) only </b></i></p><p><i><b>D (2) and (3) only</b></i></p>","answer":"<p>D</p>","reasoning":"<p>  On a longitudinal wave, the particle vibrations are parallel to the direction the wave travels. </p><p>  ∴ (1) is incorrect.</p>","kind":"multiple-choice","source":{"name":"3B04 Revision 4 Nature of Waves.docx","sha256":"ce4955eaa02f2dfff1d869d1830f48153016d479791ee42b406abae452601c85","block":23},"id":"tb-3B04-3","chapter":"3B04"},"tb-3B04-4":{"number":"4","question":"<p><i><b>A wave travels along a string to the right. The </b></i><i><b>waveform at time </b></i><i><b>t </b></i><i><b>= 0 is shown in Figure b.  </b></i><i><b>P </b></i><i><b>to </b></i><i><b>U </b></i><i><b>are particles on the string.</b></i></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-exercises/media/f48eb7722376c3a38ec8eb79.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:207.3px\"/></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>Which particles are moving in the opposite </b></i><i><b>direction as </b></i><i><b>P </b></i><i><b>at </b></i><i><b>t </b></i><i><b>= 0?</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>A </b></i><i><b>Q </b></i><i><b>and </b></i><i><b>U </b></i><i><b>only</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>B </b></i><i><b>R </b></i><i><b>and </b></i><i><b>S </b></i><i><b>only</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>C </b></i><i><b>R</b></i><i><b>, </b></i><i><b>S </b></i><i><b>and </b></i><i><b>T </b></i><i><b>only</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>D </b></i><i><b>Q</b></i><i><b>, </b></i><i><b>R </b></i><i><b>and </b></i><i><b>U </b></i><i><b>only</b></i></p>","answer":"<p>B</p>","reasoning":"<p>  At <i>t</i> = 0, </p><p>  <i>P</i>, <i>Q</i>, and <i>U</i> are moving upwards;</p><p>  <i>R</i> and <i>S</i> are moving are downwards;</p><p>  <i>T</i> is momentarily at rest.</p><p>  ∴ <i>R</i> and <i>S</i> are moving in the opposite direction as <i>P</i> at <i>t</i> = 0. </p>","kind":"multiple-choice","source":{"name":"3B04 Revision 4 Nature of Waves.docx","sha256":"ce4955eaa02f2dfff1d869d1830f48153016d479791ee42b406abae452601c85","block":35},"id":"tb-3B04-4","chapter":"3B04"},"tb-3B04-5":{"number":"5","question":"<p><i><b>A transverse travelling wave has a frequency of 5 </b></i><i><b>Hz. Its </b></i><i><b>s</b></i><i><b>–</b></i><i><b>d </b></i><i><b>graph at time </b></i><i><b>t </b></i><i><b>= 0 is shown below </b></i><i><b>(Fig c). The distance </b></i><i><b>d</b></i><i><b> is measured from the </b></i><i><b>vibrating source.</b></i></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-exercises/media/426212ae5a1ed04c84f17eea.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:252.3px\"/></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>Which of the graphs in Figure d is the correct  </b></i><i><b>s</b></i><i><b>–</b></i><i><b>t </b></i><i><b>graph of particle </b></i><i><b>P </b></i><i><b>from </b></i><i><b>t </b></i><i><b>= 0 to </b></i><i><b>t </b></i><i><b>= 0.2 s?</b></i></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-exercises/media/beb708b35a2009ccd42b4ffc.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:239.1px\"/></p>","answer":"<p>D</p>","reasoning":"<p>  At <i>t</i> = 0, <i>P</i> is at the equilibrium position and moving downwards </p>","kind":"multiple-choice","source":{"name":"3B04 Revision 4 Nature of Waves.docx","sha256":"ce4955eaa02f2dfff1d869d1830f48153016d479791ee42b406abae452601c85","block":49},"id":"tb-3B04-5","chapter":"3B04"},"tb-3B04-6":{"number":"6","question":"<p><i><b>Figure e shows the </b></i><i><b>s</b></i><i><b>–</b></i><i><b>d </b></i><i><b>graph of a travelling wave at </b></i><i><b>a certain instant. Figure f shows the </b></i><i><b>s</b></i><i><b>–</b></i><i><b>t </b></i><i><b>graph of a </b></i><i><b>particle on the wave.</b></i></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-exercises/media/6125db2f69cd661897278c98.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:236.1px\"/></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>How far does the wave travel in 4 s?</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>A 6 cm </b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>B 10 cm </b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>C 12 cm </b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>D 20 cm</b></i></p>","answer":"<p>D</p>","reasoning":"<p><b>  </b>From the <i>s</i>−<i>t</i> graph, period <i>T</i> = 8 s</p><p>  From the <i>s</i>−<i>d</i> graph, wavelength <i>λ</i> = 40 cm</p><p>  Wave speed <i>v</i> =<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>λ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>=<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>40</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>= 5 cm s<sup>−</sup><sup>1</sup></p><p>  Distance that the wave travels in 4 s</p><p>  = 5 × 4 = 20 cm</p>","kind":"multiple-choice","source":{"name":"3B04 Revision 4 Nature of Waves.docx","sha256":"ce4955eaa02f2dfff1d869d1830f48153016d479791ee42b406abae452601c85","block":56},"id":"tb-3B04-6","chapter":"3B04"},"tb-3B04-7":{"number":"7","question":"<p><i><b>The snapshots of a travelling wave at time </b></i><i><b>t </b></i><i><b>= 0 </b></i><i><b>and </b></i><i><b>t </b></i><i><b>= 0.6 s are shown below (Fig g). The wave </b></i><i><b>is travelling to the right.</b></i></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-exercises/media/4c1b25077387811275d61c5d.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:332.9px\"/></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>Which of the following statements are correct? </b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(1) The frequency of the wave is 1.25 Hz. </b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(2) </b></i><i><b>P </b></i><i><b>starts to vibrate at </b></i><i><b>t </b></i><i><b>= 0.2 s. </b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(3) The wavelength of the wave is 6 cm. </b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>A (1) and (2) only  </b></i></p><p><i><b>B (1) and (3) only </b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>C (2) and (3) only  </b></i></p><p><i><b>D (1), (2) and (3)</b></i></p>","answer":"<p>A</p>","reasoning":"<p>  The wave completes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></math> cycle in 0.6 s.</p><p>  <i>T</i> =<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0.6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>= 0.8 s</p><p>  <i>f</i> =<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></math>=<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0.8</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></math>= 1.25 Hz</p><p>  ∴ (1) is correct.</p><p>  <i>P</i> has completed half a cycle at <i>t</i> = 0.6 s. </p><p>  Let <i>X</i> be the time that <i>P</i> starts to vibrate.</p><p>    <i>X</i> +<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0.8</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></math>  = 0.6</p><p>    <i>X</i>  = 0.2 s</p><p>  <i>P</i> starts to vibrate at <i>t</i> = 0.2 s. </p><p>  ∴ (2) is correct.</p><p>    1.5<i>λ</i>  = 12 cm</p><p>    <i>λ</i>  = 8 cm</p><p>  ∴ (3) is incorrect.</p>","kind":"multiple-choice","source":{"name":"3B04 Revision 4 Nature of Waves.docx","sha256":"ce4955eaa02f2dfff1d869d1830f48153016d479791ee42b406abae452601c85","block":70},"id":"tb-3B04-7","chapter":"3B04"},"tb-3B04-8":{"number":"8","question":"<p><i><b>Figure h shows the position of particle </b></i><i><b>X </b></i><i><b>on a wave </b></i><i><b>travelling to the left at time </b></i><i><b>t </b></i><i><b>= 2 s.</b></i></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-exercises/media/02202813a79097f656d00272.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:269.9px\"/></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>Which of the following is a possible </b></i><i><b>s</b></i><i><b>–</b></i><i><b>t </b></i><i><b>graph of </b></i><i><b>X</b></i><i><b>? Take the upward direction as positive.</b></i></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-exercises/media/11b43f8f4f6b510ca66b01e2.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:277.2px\"/></p>","answer":"<p>B</p>","reasoning":"<p>  <i>X</i> is on a trough at <i>t</i> = 2 s. </p>","kind":"multiple-choice","source":{"name":"3B04 Revision 4 Nature of Waves.docx","sha256":"ce4955eaa02f2dfff1d869d1830f48153016d479791ee42b406abae452601c85","block":95},"id":"tb-3B04-8","chapter":"3B04"},"tb-3B04-9":{"number":"9","question":"<p><i><b>A wave of frequency 3 Hz travels at 12 m s</b></i><i><b><sup>−1</sup></b></i><i><b> </b></i><i><b>through a medium. The distance between the </b></i><i><b>equilibrium positions of particles </b></i><i><b>P </b></i><i><b>and </b></i><i><b>Q </b></i><i><b>on </b></i><i><b>the wave is 5 m. What is the position of </b></i><i><b>Q </b></i><i><b>when </b></i><i><b>P </b></i><i><b>is on a crest? </b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>A </b></i><i><b>On a crest </b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>B </b></i><i><b>On a trough </b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>C At its equilibrium position </b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>D None of the above</b></i></p>","answer":"<p>C</p>","reasoning":"<p>  By <i>v</i> = <i>f</i><i>λ</i>, </p><p>    12  = (3)<i>λ</i></p><p>    <i>λ</i>  = 4 m</p><p>  The distance between the equilibrium positions of <i>P</i> and <i>Q</i> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></math><i>λ</i>. Therefore, <i>Q</i> is at its equilibrium position when <i>P</i> is on a crest. </p>","kind":"multiple-choice","source":{"name":"3B04 Revision 4 Nature of Waves.docx","sha256":"ce4955eaa02f2dfff1d869d1830f48153016d479791ee42b406abae452601c85","block":102},"id":"tb-3B04-9","chapter":"3B04"},"tb-3B04-10":{"number":"10","question":"<p><i><b>A bird lands on a floating buoy. The buoy starts to </b></i><i><b>produce a wave at time </b></i><i><b>t </b></i><i><b>= 0. Figure i shows a </b></i><i><b>snapshot of the wave at </b></i><i><b>t </b></i><i><b>= 0.6 s.</b></i></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-exercises/media/02597d773c3f33926c3e14cb.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:303.6px\"/></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b> Particle </b></i><i><b>P </b></i><i><b>first reaches its highest position at  </b></i><i><b>t </b></i><i><b>= 1.8 s. What is the distance </b></i><i><b>x </b></i><i><b>between </b></i><i><b>P </b></i><i><b>and </b></i><i><b>the buoy? </b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>A </b></i><i><b>2.4 m  </b></i></p><p><i><b>B 3.0 m </b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>C 3.6 m  </b></i></p><p><i><b>D 4.2 m</b></i></p>","answer":"<p>B</p>","reasoning":"<p>  <i>v</i> =<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></math>=<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1.2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0.6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></math>= 2 m s<sup>–1</sup> </p><p>    1.5<i>λ</i>  = 1.2 m</p><p>    <i>λ</i>  = 0.8 m</p><p>  The wave has travelled pass <i>P</i> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></math>wavelength when <i>P</i> first reaches its highest position. </p><p>    <i>d</i>  = <i>vt</i></p><p>    <i>x</i> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>× 0.8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>  = (2)(1.8)</p><p>    <i>x</i>  = 3.0 m</p>","kind":"multiple-choice","source":{"name":"3B04 Revision 4 Nature of Waves.docx","sha256":"ce4955eaa02f2dfff1d869d1830f48153016d479791ee42b406abae452601c85","block":113},"id":"tb-3B04-10","chapter":"3B04"},"tb-3B04-14":{"number":"14","question":"<p><i><b>A student stretches a slinky spring to a length  </b></i><i><b>of 5 m. One end of the spring is fixed. She flicks </b></i><i><b>the other end and observes that the transverse </b></i><i><b>pulse takes 2 s to travel to the fixed end. </b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(a) Calculate the speed of the transverse  </b></i><i><b>pulse along the spring. </b></i><i><b>(1 mark)</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(b) If the student flicks the spring continuously </b></i><i><b>at 4 Hz, what is the wavelength of the wave </b></i><i><b>produced? </b></i><i><b>(2 marks)</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(c) Suggest </b></i><i><b>two </b></i><i><b>methods to produce a wave of </b></i><i><b>shorter wavelength on the spring. Explain </b></i><i><b>your answer. </b></i><i><b>(4 marks)</b></i></p>","answer":"<p>  (a)  <i>v</i> =<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></math>=<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></math>= 2.5 m s<sup>–1</sup>   1A</p><p>  (b)  By <i>v</i> = <i>f</i><i>λ</i>, </p><p>      2.5  = (4)<i>λ</i>  1M</p><p>      <i>λ</i>  = 0.625 m  1A</p><p>  (c)  Method 1:</p><p>    Decrease the tension in the spring or use a heavier spring.  1A</p><p>    This can reduce the wave speed. By <i>v</i> = <i>f</i><i>λ</i>, the wavelength <i>λ</i><i> </i>decreases as the wave speed <i>v</i> decreases.   1A</p><p>    Method 2:</p><p>    Increase the frequency of the wave.  1A</p><p>    By <i>v</i> = <i>f</i><i>λ</i>, the wavelength <i>λ</i> decreases when the frequency <i>f</i> increases.  1A</p>","reasoning":"","kind":"structured","source":{"name":"3B04 Revision 4 Nature of Waves.docx","sha256":"ce4955eaa02f2dfff1d869d1830f48153016d479791ee42b406abae452601c85","block":129},"id":"tb-3B04-14","chapter":"3B04"},"tb-3B04-15":{"number":"15","question":"<p><i><b>Figure l shows a water wave travelling to the right. </b></i><i><b>The crest at </b></i><i><b>X</b></i><i><b> takes 2 s to reach </b></i><i><b>Y</b></i><i><b>. </b></i><i><b>P</b></i><i><b> and </b></i><i><b>Q</b></i><i><b> </b></i><i><b>are two particles floating on the water. They </b></i><i><b>are moving at the highest speed at the instant </b></i><i><b>shown.</b></i></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-exercises/media/f8dbd0a461bddd51d89e1874.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:340.3px\"/></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(a) Find the wave speed. </b></i><i><b>(2 marks)</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(b) Find the frequency of the wave. </b></i><i><b>(2 marks)</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(c) Describe the motion of </b></i><i><b>P</b></i><i><b> and </b></i><i><b>Q</b></i><i><b> at the </b></i><i><b>instant shown. </b></i><i><b>(2 marks)</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(d) How long will it take for </b></i><i><b>P</b></i><i><b> to first become </b></i><i><b>momentarily stationary? </b></i><i><b>(2 marks)</b></i></p>","answer":"<p>  (a)  Wave speed <i>v</i> =<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>12</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></math>  1M</p><p>      = 6 m s<sup>−</sup><sup>1</sup>   1A</p><p>  (b)  Period <i>T</i> =<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></math>= 0.667 s  1M</p><p>    Frequency <i>f</i> =<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></math>=<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0.667</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></math>= 1.50 Hz  1A</p><p>  (c)  <i>P</i>: Moving upwards  1A</p><p>    <i>Q</i>: Moving downwards  1A</p><p>  (d)  <i>P</i> is at its equilibrium position.   It will first become momentarily at rest <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></math> period later.</p><p>    <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></math><i>T</i> =<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></math>(0.667)   1M</p><p>      = 0.167 s   1A</p><p>    <i>P</i> will first become momentarily at rest 0.167 s later</p>","reasoning":"","kind":"structured","source":{"name":"3B04 Revision 4 Nature of Waves.docx","sha256":"ce4955eaa02f2dfff1d869d1830f48153016d479791ee42b406abae452601c85","block":146},"id":"tb-3B04-15","chapter":"3B04"},"tb-3B04-16":{"number":"16","question":"<p><i><b>Figures m and n show the </b></i><i><b>s</b></i><i><b>–</b></i><i><b>d </b></i><i><b>graphs of a </b></i><i><b>transverse wave at time </b></i><i><b>t </b></i><i><b>= 0 and </b></i><i><b>t </b></i><i><b>= 0.2 s </b></i><i><b>respectively. </b></i><i><b>X</b></i><i><b>, </b></i><i><b>Y </b></i><i><b>and </b></i><i><b>Z </b></i><i><b>are three particles on </b></i><i><b>the wave.</b></i></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-exercises/media/209ffe1b514b7a312cd08008.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:294.7px\"/></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(a) Find the amplitude, wavelength, wave speed </b></i><i><b>and frequency of the wave. </b></i><i><b>(4 marks)</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(b) Which particle, </b></i><i><b>X</b></i><i><b>, </b></i><i><b>Y </b></i><i><b>or </b></i><i><b>Z</b></i><i><b>, is on a wave crest </b></i><i><b>at </b></i><i><b>t </b></i><i><b>= 0.6 s? </b></i><i><b>(1 mark)</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(c) Describe the motion of </b></i><i><b>X</b></i><i><b>, </b></i><i><b>Y </b></i><i><b>and </b></i><i><b>Z </b></i><i><b>at  </b></i><i><b>t </b></i><i><b>= 0.5 s. </b></i><i><b>(3 marks)</b></i></p>","answer":"<p>  (a)  Amplitude = 15 cm  1A</p><p>    Wavelength = 2 m  1A</p><p>    Wave speed =<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></math>=<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3−2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0.2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></math>= 5 m s<sup>–1</sup>  1A</p><p>    The wave completes a half cycle in 0.2 s. </p><p>    Frequency =<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0.5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0.2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></math>= 2.5 Hz  1A</p><p>  (b)  <i>X</i>  1A</p><p>  (c)  </p><p><img src=\"assets/textbook-exercises/media/863aab5017f1a38eba686cc2.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:300.7px\"/></p><p>    <i>X</i>: moving upwards  1A</p><p>    <i>Y</i>: momentarily at rest  1A</p><p>  <i>  </i><i>Z</i>: moving downwards  1A</p>","reasoning":"","kind":"structured","source":{"name":"3B04 Revision 4 Nature of Waves.docx","sha256":"ce4955eaa02f2dfff1d869d1830f48153016d479791ee42b406abae452601c85","block":164},"id":"tb-3B04-16","chapter":"3B04"},"tb-3B04-17":{"number":"17","question":"<p><i><b>A transverse wave is produced in water and travels </b></i><i><b>to the right. Figure o shows the waveform of the </b></i><i><b>wave at time </b></i><i><b>t </b></i><i><b>= 0 and </b></i><i><b>t </b></i><i><b>= 0.5 s.</b></i></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"a