B04-17","chapter":"3B04"},"tb-3B04-18":{"number":"18","question":"<p><i><b>Figure p shows a transverse travelling wave along </b></i><i><b>a spring at time </b></i><i><b>t </b></i><i><b>= 0.5 s. Particle </b></i><i><b>X </b></i><i><b>on the wave </b></i><i><b>completes 20 cycles of vibration in 4 s.</b></i></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-exercises/media/2411380cf569de14d57fe452.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:278.2px\"/></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(a) Particles </b></i><i><b>X </b></i><i><b>and </b></i><i><b>Y </b></i><i><b>on the wave vibrate in </b></i><i><b>antiphase. Label all possible positions for </b></i><i><b>particle </b></i><i><b>Y </b></i><i><b>in Figure p. </b></i><i><b>(1 mark)</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(b) Find the period of the wave. </b></i><i><b>(2 marks)</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(c) The minimum possible separation between </b></i><i><b>the equilibrium positions of </b></i><i><b>X </b></i><i><b>and </b></i><i><b>Y </b></i><i><b>is  </b></i><i><b>0.6 m. Find the speed of the wave. (2 marks)</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(d) Sketch the </b></i><i><b>s</b></i><i><b>–</b></i><i><b>t</b></i><i><b> graph for </b></i><i><b>X</b></i><i><b> from </b></i><i><b>t</b></i><i><b> = 0  </b></i><i><b>to </b></i><i><b>t</b></i><i><b> = 0.5 s. </b></i><i><b>(2 marks)</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(e) Sketch a graph to show how the kinetic </b></i><i><b>energy of </b></i><i><b>X</b></i><i><b> changes from </b></i><i><b>t</b></i><i><b> = 0 to </b></i><i><b>t</b></i><i><b> = 0.5 s.</b></i> <i><b> </b></i><i><b>(3 marks)</b></i></p>","answer":"<p>  (a)  </p><p class=\"study-figure\">    <img src=\"assets/textbook-exercises/media/0e47f243da4edddcf205077d.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:227.3px\"/></p><p>    (Correct points marked)  1A</p><p>  (b)  Period =<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>20</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></math>  1M</p><p>      = 0.2 s  1A</p><p>  (c)    <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></math><i>λ</i>  = 0.6 m</p><p>      <i>λ</i>  = 1.2 m  1M</p><p>    <i>v</i> =<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>λ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></math>=<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1.2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0.2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></math>= 6 m s<sup>–1</sup>  1A</p><div class=\"study-table-wrap\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p>  (d)</p></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>    (Correct period)  1A</p><p>    (Starting from a crest)  1A</p><div class=\"study-table-wrap\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p>  (e)</p></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>    (Correct period)  1A</p><p>    (Starting from Zero)  1A</p><p>    (Correct axes)  1A</p>","reasoning":"","kind":"structured","source":{"name":"3B04 Revision 4 Nature of Waves.docx","sha256":"ce4955eaa02f2dfff1d869d1830f48153016d479791ee42b406abae452601c85","block":196},"id":"tb-3B04-18","chapter":"3B04"},"tb-3B04-19":{"number":"19","question":"<p><i><b>You are provided with a long spring and a stop-</b></i><i><b>watch (Fig q). </b></i></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-exercises/media/5b5d5b60955177138f714908.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:264.0px\"/></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(a) Describe how you can use the apparatus to </b></i><i><b>demonstrate that wave speed is independent </b></i><i><b>of the amplitude of a wave. </b></i><i><b>(5 marks)</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(b) Describe how you can increase the wave </b></i><i><b>speed along the spring. </b></i><i><b>(1 mark)</b></i></p>","answer":"<p>  (a)  Stretch the long spring to a fixed length.   1A</p><p><b>  </b><b>  </b>Flick one end of the spring to produce a pulse.  1A</p><p>    Measure the time <i>t</i> needed for the pulse to travel from one end to the other using the stop-watch.  1A</p><p>    Repeat the experiment by flicking the spring with different amplitudes.  1A</p><p>    The time recorded should be roughly the same.   1A</p><p>  (b)  Stretch the spring to a longer length. <br/>  1A</p>","reasoning":"","kind":"structured","source":{"name":"3B04 Revision 4 Nature of Waves.docx","sha256":"ce4955eaa02f2dfff1d869d1830f48153016d479791ee42b406abae452601c85","block":220},"id":"tb-3B04-19","chapter":"3B04"},"tb-3B04-20":{"number":"20","question":"<p><i><b>Figure r shows the waveform of a transverse </b></i><i><b>wave at time </b></i><i><b>t</b></i><i><b> = 1 s. The wave travels to the </b></i><i><b>right and has a period of 4 s.</b></i></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-exercises/media/7b32902fb928409ba21cce08.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:315.8px\"/></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(a) Which particles are moving in the same </b></i><i><b>direction as particle </b></i><i><b>C</b></i><i><b> at </b></i><i><b>t</b></i><i><b> = 1 s? </b></i><i><b>(4 marks)</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(b) Sketch the displacement–time graph of </b></i><i><b>particle </b></i><i><b>C</b></i><i><b> from </b></i><i><b>t</b></i><i><b> = 0 to </b></i><i><b>t</b></i><i><b> = 5 s. </b></i><i><b>(3 marks)</b></i></p>","answer":"<p>  (a)  <i>B</i>, <i>D</i>, <i>J</i>, <i>K</i>  4 × 1A</p><p>  (b)  </p><p>    (Correct period)  1A</p><p>    (Starting from a trough)  1A</p><p>    (Correct axes)  1A</p>","reasoning":"","kind":"structured","source":{"name":"3B04 Revision 4 Nature of Waves.docx","sha256":"ce4955eaa02f2dfff1d869d1830f48153016d479791ee42b406abae452601c85","block":232},"id":"tb-3B04-20","chapter":"3B04"},"tb-3B04-21":{"number":"21","question":"<p><i><b>A string 